{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310800,"nonce":"gpyj9ST6b1mb9udC43G1Yt6BjH8=","title":"The mandelbrot checksum oracle — rayzor's 112798288 sits at the FMA-contracted end of a measured band, verified against Haxe/Python/C","message":"Measured 2026-08-13 on the real benchmark (875x500, MaxIterations 1000). Use\nthese as the oracle instead of re-deriving; the whole question is settled.\n\n| implementation | checksum | sumIterations |\n|---|---|---|\n| Haxe `--interp` (same source) | 112790102 | 97931565 |\n| Python 3 (strict IEEE) | 112790102 | — |\n| C `clang -O2 -ffp-contract=off` | 112790102 | 97931565 |\n| **C `-ffp-contract=fast`** | **112798531** | 97811109 |\n| rayzor before phi-SRA | 112798515 | — |\n| **rayzor HEAD (Cranelift AND LLVM)** | **112798288** | 97811585 |\n| rayzor tier-0 interpreted | 110877786 | — |\n\n**The contraction band is [112790102, 112798531], 8429 wide.** Rayzor sits at\nthe contracted end, 243 from the C `-ffp-contract=fast` reference (16 before\nphi-SRA). So the phi-SRA move of 227 is 2.7% of the band and is a legitimate\nchange of contraction schedule: promoting values into registers changes which\nmul/add pairs land in one block and get fused. Cranelift and LLVM agree\nEXACTLY, which is the property that matters for [[project_nue_ml_framework]].\n\nTRAPS:\n- Do NOT claim \"within the contraction band\" without measuring the band. The\n  numbers 97931565 / 97810722 in older notes are **sumIterations, not\n  checksums** — mixing them up is how the claim got asserted rather than shown.\n- Hand-rolled `fma()` in selected spots does NOT reproduce it: single-site\n  variants land within ~500 of the uncontracted value, nowhere near the\n  contracted one. Use whole-program `-ffp-contract=fast`, not fma() by hand.\n- 112789639 (quoted in an older session as a reference) is the C variant with\n  ONLY the square-imaginary term contracted. It is not an authority.\n- The tier-0 number is 1.9M off because interpreter Math.* is broken — see\n  [[bugs_interpreter_ffi_passes_floats_in_int_registers]]. Never use the\n  interpreter as an FP oracle.\n- Repro sources: `scratchpad/mandel_ref.py`, `scratchpad/mandel_fma2.c`\n  (mode bitmask selects which terms use `fma()`; mode 0 + compiler flag is what\n  you want).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_mandelbrot_checksum_verified_against_references` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}